---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const cycleCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Mitigation (before, always)', note: 'Reduce the risk itself: levees, building codes, land-use rules. Every mitigation dollar returns multiples in avoided losses.' },
    { math: '2 · Preparedness (before, near)', note: 'Plans, exercises, stockpiles, training — readiness for the response that mitigation could not make unnecessary.' },
    { math: '3 · Response (during)', note: 'Save lives, stabilise the scene, restore essentials. Organised under ICS so that dozens of agencies interoperate without improvising hierarchy.' },
    { math: '4 · Recovery (after, for years)', note: 'Debris, housing, infrastructure, economy — the longest phase, where resilience either improves or repeats.' },
    { math: '5 · The wheel turns', note: 'Every recovery should feed mitigation: build back better, or build back the same vulnerability. The cycle is the discipline\'s core idea.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/emergency-management/foundational/the-emergency-management-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Emergency management is the professional discipline of disasters — and its core concept is
    the cycle: mitigation (reducing risk), preparedness (building capability), response (managing
    the event) and recovery (rebuilding afterwards). Each phase feeds the next; the whole field is
    organised to make the wheel turn faster and wiser.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four phases and how they feed each other" widget="equationStep" config={cycleCfg} />

  <h2>Mitigation is the quiet champion</h2>
  <p>
    The cheapest disaster is the one that never happens. FEMA's own analyses found that every
    federal mitigation dollar saved roughly six in future losses — building codes, floodplain
    management and elevation projects do their work invisibly, decades before the storm. Because
    its successes are absences, mitigation is chronically underfunded; the discipline's advocacy
    exists to correct that bias.
  </p>

  <h2>Response is governed, not improvised</h2>
  <p>
    The instinct after catastrophe is heroic improvisation; the doctrine is the opposite. The
    Incident Command System (ICS) gives any incident a standard structure — command, operations,
    planning, logistics, finance — so a fire department from one state can slot into a unified
    command with utilities and the National Guard from another. NIMS makes interagency chaos a
    solvable paperwork problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Recovery is where equity shows</h2>
  <p>
    Disasters redistribute harm unevenly: the households least able to absorb losses recover
    slowest, and rebuilding decisions decide who returns. Modern emergency management treats
    equity as an operational variable — who gets aid, how fast, with what conditions — because
    the recovery phase lasts years and its politics decide whether the community that emerges is
    more resilient, or just the same.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FEMA EMI curriculum (NIMS, IS courses); IAEM CEM competencies; multi-hazard mitigation council benefit-cost analyses.</p>
</TopicPage>
